00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.020
you need to be running
in Firestore Native mode.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.380
Similarly, when prompted to
create a new NoSQL Datastore,

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.140
you get to choose between
Firestore in Datastore mode

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.180
or Firestore in Native mode.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.010
Check out the chart
in the docs to see

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.660
the differences between them.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:49.780
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, I see.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.590
So all users need to do
is switch from Firestore

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:58.050
in Datastore mode to Native
mode, and they're good, right?

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.850
But I think I better ask now.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:01.530
What's the bad
news you mentioned?

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:02.280
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.560
I think you know what
I'm going to say.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.770
You can't switch between
Datastore and Native Firestore

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:08.460
modes.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:12.180
Once the mode is set and data
is stored, the mode is locked.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.540
Also, to compound matters,
Datastore and Firestore

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.210
are mutually exclusive
in GCP projects,

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.920
meaning that once
the mode is locked,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.760
not only can you not change it.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.310
But in order to
use the other mode,

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.908
you need to actually
create a brand new project.

00:03:25.908 --> 00:03:27.450
MARTIN OMANDER: So
what you're saying

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.060
is if I've got
data in Datastore,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.300
even if I migrate to the Cloud
NDB or Cloud Datastore client

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.830
libraries, I'm not able to use
