00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.250
to Firestore in the new project.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:42.520
That's part one.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.580
Secondly, it probably
goes without saying

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.730
that you need an app in that new
project that uses the Firestore

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:49.500
client library.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.280
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, I got it.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.450
But before we do
that, I remember from,

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.480
was it, module 3 that the
Cloud Datastore client

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:01.940
library can be used by both App
Engine and non-App Engine apps.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.688
Is that true for Cloud
Firestore, as well?

00:05:04.688 --> 00:05:06.230
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah,
definitely Martin.

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:08.720
While we're going to demo
migrating an App Engine

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.690
app to Cloud Firestore, it
could be any app, really.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:13.460
The only real
requirement is that you

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.020
have to have a new project.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:17.240
And since you
mentioned module 3,

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.850
that's where we created
our Cloud Datastore app.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.550
So we're going to migrate
that one to Cloud Firestore.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:25.880
Specifically the Python 3
version, as Python 2 apps

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:28.740
are more likely to be
using Cloud NDB instead.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.680
So if you did the
codelab, use your code,

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.500
or grab ours from
the module 3b folder

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.380
by either cloning the repo
or downloading the zip file.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:37.850
Pause here if you need to.

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:41.880
But once you're ready, let's
go to the computer now.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:44.130
OK, before we start, we
need another Cloud project

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:45.090
for Firestore.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:46.980
So make a new one in
the Cloud Console,

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:50.040
or reuse an existing one where
neither Datastore nor Firestore

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:50.940
are used.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:52.470
Grab that project's ID--

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.960
not the project name,
nor number, the ID.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.510
Then enable the
Cloud Firestore API.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:00.780
If you prefer the command line,
use gcloud projects create,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.960
then change to that new
project with gcloud config set,

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:07.620
and finally, turn on Firestore
with gcloud services enable.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:10.170
Of course, you'll be asked
to enable billing, as well.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:12.870
Since we always suggest you
start with working code,

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.330
deploy the module
3 Python 3 app,

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.100
whether you're
using yours or ours.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:19.110
But do this from
your old project,

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.460
the one using Datastore.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.550
Use gcloud config list to
see your current project,

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.350
and use gcloud config
set, if necessary.

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:28.320
Once you're on your
Datastore project,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.170
run gcloud app deploy
and confirm the app works

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:32.010
like before.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:35.520
Now switch to your new project
with gcloud config set project

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.090
one more time, as we'll be here
the rest of this migration,

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.850
starting with the
configuration files.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.590
The only config
you need to change

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:46.290
is to switch from Cloud
Datastore, or Cloud NDB

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.900
if you're using that,
to Cloud Firestore.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:52.200
There should be a one-line
swap in requirements.txt.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.570
All other changes
are in main.py.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:57.240
Replace the Cloud Datastore
client library imports

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:01.050
and API client instantiation at
the top with the same for Cloud

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:01.710
Firestore.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:03.750
Groups of like
entities in Datastore

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:06.240
are identified with
a common parent key.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:07.950
Entities in Datastore
are referred

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:10.110
to as documents in Firestore.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:12.930
Entities are of a certain
kind in Datastore,

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:16.080
while Firestore documents
are grouped as collections.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.300
When storing a new
visit in Datastore,

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.390
a new entity is created
with the visit parent key.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:23.430
Likewise on the
Firestore side, we

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:26.820
set our collection to the same
name as a Datastore parent key.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.820
The Datastore entity gets
its data key-value pairs

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:32.380
in the same way that a
Firestore document does,

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:34.260
but the latter gets
added to the collection

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:36.720
immediately, while the
Datastore entity has

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:38.460
to be written explicitly.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:40.290
Next are the Datastore
queries where

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:43.980
you query for visit entity
kinds, the desired ordering,

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.420
then fetch and return.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.210
For Firestore, query
the visit collection,

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.460
specify the ordering,
fetch and convert

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:52.350
to Python-friendly
dictionaries, then

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:54.600
return as a
memory-friendly iterable.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.730
Believe it or not,
that wraps the changes

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:59.970
necessary to switch from Cloud
Datastore to Cloud Firestore.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:02.770
The real work is in having
to create a new project.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:06.502
So what do you think of
that migration, Martin?

00:08:06.502 --> 00:08:08.710
MARTIN OMANDER: That migration
wasn't too bad at all.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:13.030
But earlier, you said any
app can use Cloud Firestore.

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:16.330
If I'm willing to create a new
project to access Firestore

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:19.420
and take the effort to
migrate my data over,

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:22.180
does this mean I can rewrite
my Python 2 Datastore

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:26.237
app as a Node.js Firestore app?

00:08:26.237 --> 00:08:27.570
WESLEY CHUN: Absolutely, Martin.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.240
Your Datastore app could
be App Engine, Cloud Run,

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:31.440
or even non-Cloud.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:33.330
It's not like the
old App Engine days,

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:36.030
where if you're using Datastore,
it had to be an App Engine app.

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:37.870
That's certainly not
the case anymore.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.653
So the same applies
to your new project,

00:08:39.653 --> 00:08:41.070
meaning you can
have any app using

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:42.940
Firestore as its database.

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:46.270
So if your company is doing
more work in Node.js today,

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:48.450
there's nothing wrong with
rebuilding or refactoring

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:51.900
as a Node 16 Cloud Run app for
your new Firestore project.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:54.090
That's what we mean by
App Engine, and really

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:55.770
all of Google Cloud
as a whole, being

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.440
more open than ever
before, for both your data

00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.538
and for your apps.

00:08:59.538 --> 00:09:00.330
MARTIN OMANDER: Hm.

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:02.050
Well, that's really interesting.

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:03.690
I've been thinking
about doing exactly

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:07.950
that, as my team is doing
more with Node.js these days.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:11.610
Anyway, now that I know how to
migrate my app to Firestore,

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:13.937
what about migrating my data?

00:09:13.937 --> 00:09:15.270
WESLEY CHUN: Definitely, Martin.

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:17.460
We're going to tackle
that in module 10.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:20.310
While you're awaiting for
the data migration sequel,

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:23.340
here are some additional
resources to consider.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:26.520
Earlier, we linked you to a
chart differentiating Datastore

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.080
and Firestore Native modes.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.900
Above that chart is our
guidance on which of those modes

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.600
to choose for your apps, so
check that page in the docs

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.880
and just look over all
of the Firestore docs,

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:39.960
anyway, to become familiar with
your brand new NoSQL database.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.420
Also, check out this other
video from one of our colleagues

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.240
that dives deeper into the
migration and the differences

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:47.155
between Datastore and Firestore.

00:09:47.155 --> 00:09:48.030
MARTIN OMANDER: Cool.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:49.660
I'll check those out.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:51.480
Now are there other
migrations I should

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:53.002
consider for my App Engine app?

00:09:53.002 --> 00:09:55.210
WESLEY CHUN: Well, first,
you can see from this list,

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:57.127
we've already migrated
from our old App Engine

00:09:57.127 --> 00:10:01.590
Datastore and NDB to Cloud
NDB, Cloud Datastore, and now

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:02.850
Cloud Firestore.

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:04.920
However, if you're
on Cloud NDB, you

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.140
can migrate directly
to Cloud Firestore.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.570
There's no need to migrate
first to Cloud Datastore

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:11.370
and then go from
Datastore to Firestore.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:14.430
just combine module
3 with this module 6.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:16.500
As far as other
migrations go, if you're

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:18.480
thinking about containerizing
your App Engine

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:22.560
apps to run on Cloud Run, go
and check out modules 4 and 5.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.690
Modules 7, 8, and
9 help you learn

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:26.880
about how to migrate from
App Engine Task Queue

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.380
to Cloud Tasks.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:31.260
Finally, if you've got a small
app that's just one function,

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:32.910
